This directory contains an MFEM example focused on benchmarking MAGMA batched
element-mass-matrix inverses/solves on GPU backends (CUDA/HIP).

- `batched_mass_bench.cpp`: Assemble packed element mass matrices and compare:
  - MFEM tripack inverse apply (packed lower-triangular)
  - MAGMA packed Cholesky factor + solve
  - MAGMA packed inverse (ppinv) apply (when elem_dofs <= 64)
  - MFEM BatchedLinAlg MAGMA full (dense) LU factor + solve
  - MAGMA full (dense) inverse + apply

Build and run from this directory (after building MFEM with MAGMA enabled):

- `make batched_mass_bench`
- `./batched_mass_bench -m ../../data/beam-hex.mesh -o 3 -r 2 -d gpu -reps 100`

Plotting / throughput curves
----------------------------

The script `plot_magma_results.py` can sweep orders/refinements, collect
timings, and generate setup/apply/total speedup plots. It also writes CSV and
JSONL timing data.

If you only want raw timing breakdowns in a tidy "long" table (kernel-by-kernel
rows with assembly/factorization/apply), use `collect_magma_timings.py`.

Examples:

Sweep orders 1, 2, and 3 in 3D, using 100 RHS/apply repetitions:

- `python3 plot_magma_results.py --devices hip --orders 1,2,3 --reps 100`
- `python3 collect_magma_timings.py --devices hip --orders 1,2,3 --reps 100 --trials 3`
- `python3 collect_magma_timings.py --devices hip --orders 1,2,3 --reps 100 --trials 3 --plot` (requires matplotlib)

Notes:
- By default, speedups are relative to the MFEM BatchedLinAlg MAGMA full LU
  baseline. Use `--baseline tripack` to normalize against MFEM tripack instead.
- If `matplotlib` is not available, the script still writes CSV/JSONL data but
  skips PNG generation.
